        "surveyDate": "1987-07-15",
        "surveyType": "Standard Survey",
        "narrative": "<p>WALLEYE ABUNDANCE IS LOW, AS IT WAS IN 1985, AND HAS DECLINED SHARPLY SINCE THE 1972 ASSESSMENT. AVERAGE SIZE APPEARS TO BE GOOD. THE SAMPLE SIZE IS TO SMALL TO DRAW ANY CONCLUSIONS ABOUT YEAR- CLASS STRENGTH. WALLEYE GROWTH IS GOOD, SOMEWHAT ABOVE STATE AVERAGE AT AGE IV. SMALLMOUTH BASS ABUNDANCE AND AVERAGE SIZE ARE GOOD, BUT GROWTH APPEARS TO BE SLOW. LAKE WHITEFISH ABUNDANCE HAS DECLINED DRASTICALLY SINCE 1972, AND NORTHERN CISCO HAVE REPLACED THEM AS THE DOMINANT COREGONID IN THE LAKE. SINCE CISCO ARE GENERALLY A BETTER FORAGE SPECIES THAN LAKE WHITEFISH, THIS COULD BE SEEN AS A POSITIVE CHANGE IF NOT FOR THE COINDIDING DROP IN WALLEYE ABUNDANCE OVER THE SAME PERIOD.</p>",
